# FERROR(3S)

## NAME

feof, ferror, clearerr, fileno - stream status inquiries

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int feof(FILE *stream);

int ferror(FILE *stream);

void clearerr(FILE *stream);

int fileno(FILE *stream);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Feof* returns non-zero when end of file is read on the named input *stream,* otherwise zero.

*Ferror* returns non-zero when an error has occurred reading or writing the named *stream,*
otherwise zero. Unless cleared by *clearerr,* the error indication lasts until the stream is
closed.

*Clearerr* resets the error indication on the named *stream.* **Note:** v7's own page spelled
it *clrerr* in the text and *clearerr* in the synopsis. The name is *clearerr*; the source file
is called *clrerr.c* because the object directory is flat.

*Fileno* returns the integer file descriptor associated with the *stream,* see open(2). It is
POSIX rather than C11, and is here because v7 has it and v7 code uses it.

These functions are implemented as macros; they cannot be redeclared. **Note:** *feof*,
*ferror* and *clearerr* also exist as real functions, which is C11 sec 7.1.4 - a macro may be
suppressed by parenthesising the name, and there must be something to call when it is. Each of
the three evaluates its argument exactly once, unlike getc(3) and putc(3).

## SEE ALSO

fopen(3), open(2), stdio(3)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/stdio/feof.c, ferror.c, clrerr.c
